    card title "Most women in parliament?"

A: Which country has the most women in its parliament? Four suspects, two clues: guess along.
    draw flags

A: Clue one: more than six in ten of its members are women.
    draw clue1

B: Six in ten? Sweden. It's always Sweden.
    point at.ref fl_swe gesture circle

A: Clue two: in 2008 it became the first parliament anywhere with more women than men.
    draw clue2

    ask "Tap the country you think it is." choose ["g_swe","g_isl","g_rwa","g_cub"] answer g_rwa
        right: Rwanda: about sixty-four percent women, the highest share in the world.
        wrong: Look beyond Europe.

    erase clue1 clue2

B: Sweden only forty-five? I'd have bet the house on it.
    draw sh_swe sh_isl sh_cub sh_rwa parallel true

    erase flags sh_swe sh_isl sh_cub sh_rwa

A: Picture Rwanda's parliament as a hundred seats: sixty-four held by women.
    draw rw rw_t

A: Now the world's parliaments, on average: twenty-seven.
    draw wo wo_t

A: How did Rwanda get there? After the 1994 genocide, its 2 (@draw quota@)003 constitution reserved at least thirty percent of seats for women, and women went on to win far more.

B: So it's the model democracy, then.
    highlight rw_t

    erase quota

A: Not by V-Dem's ratings: Rwanda counts as an electoral autocracy, and Cuba, in second place, a closed one.
    text auto "V-Dem: electoral autocracy" #8a5fa8 cites ["regime"] x 500 y 222 font_size 46

A: So count the seats, then ask what those seats can decide.
    highlight auto

